  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Prove that\n$$\\sum_{d\\mid n}\\phi(d)=n$$\nwhere the sum is over all positive divisors $d$ of $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle\\sum_{d\\mid n}\\phi(d)=n$.\n\nCount the $n$ fractions $\\frac{a}{n}$, with $1\\le a\\le n$, according to their denominators after reduction to least terms.",
      "worked": "Consider the $n$ fractions $\\frac{a}{n}$, where $1\\le a\\le n$. Each reduces uniquely to $\\frac{b}{d}$, where $d\\mid n$, $1\\le b\\le d$ and $\\gcd(b,d)=1$.\n\nFor each divisor $d$ of $n$, the reduced fractions with denominator $d$ correspond exactly to the $\\phi(d)$ possible numerators $b$. Indeed, each such fraction arises from the original numerator $a=\\frac{bn}{d}$.\n\nThus grouping the original fractions by their reduced denominators counts every fraction exactly once. Therefore,\n$$\\sum_{d\\mid n}\\phi(d)=n.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
